<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

  The dialplan context every aicc-managed SIP account lives in.

  aicc_xml.lua puts each account it serves into this context, so everything an
  agent phone can dial is decided here and nowhere else. That is the whole
  point: the stock dialplan is left untouched, and this file is the only place
  a reader has to look to know what an extension reaches.

  It replaces an earlier arrangement in which agents sat in the stock `default`
  context and extension-to-extension dialling was left to the stock
  `Local_Extension` rule (design 01 §7 D6). That rule works as telephony and
  fails as a call centre: the legs it raises carry none of the aicc variables,
  so the application sees an unattributed pair of channels, mints a call of its
  own around them and gets both the direction and the roles wrong — the agent
  being rung was shown "Calling out" and their caller's leg. It also falls
  through to voicemail on no-answer, and each successor leg becomes its own CDR
  row (C24). Neither is a thing to patch around downstream; both disappear by
  not routing aicc calls through rules that were never about aicc.

  Nothing from the stock context is inherited. Feature codes an agent phone
  never uses — call pickup, park, redial, the voicemail keys — are absent by
  choice rather than by oversight. Anything an agent genuinely needs is added
  here explicitly, which keeps the reachable surface of a call centre phone
  something you can read in one screen.
-->
<include>
  <context name="aicc">

    <!--
      The switch's own end to a call that outlasted the deployment's maximum
      duration (#637). Nothing dials these: a call is sent here by a task the
      switch scheduled on the call's own channel, so the call still ends when
      the application is dead or stuck.

        aicc_max_call_duration_bot     the part of a call a bot serves. Scheduled
                                       when the customer answers an AI call:
                                       on the originate of an AI outbound leg,
                                       and by aicc_inbound.lua for every DID.
                                       It fires AICC_BOT_MAX_CALL_DURATION plus
                                       a 60 s grace after the answer, so the
                                       application's own ending runs first.
        aicc_max_call_duration_agent   the part an agent serves. Scheduled by
                                       aicc_queue.lua at the call's first queue
                                       entry (later entries keep that deadline),
                                       and on the agent leg of a click-to-dial
                                       at the agent's answer, at
                                       AICC_AGENT_MAX_CALL_DURATION.

      The task is a transfer here, not sched_hangup, because the mark has to be
      stamped when the kill happens and only then: sched_hangup reads a cause
      it does not know as NORMAL_CLEARING without a word, its default is the
      ALLOTTED_TIMEOUT this product already uses for a late answer, and nothing
      in a hangup event says the scheduler did it. aicc_hangup_cause replaces
      the switch's cause in the application (OVERFLOW rides the same variable),
      and the side says which cap it was. A partner leg in a bridge with this
      one is hung up when this leg leaves the bridge, so the whole call ends.

      First in the context on purpose: nothing above can match the name first,
      and no rule a deployment adds through the include below is reached
      before it.
    -->
    <extension name="aicc_max_call_duration_bot">
      <condition field="destination_number" expression="^aicc_max_call_duration_bot$">
        <action application="set" data="aicc_hangup_cause=MAX_CALL_DURATION"/>
        <action application="set" data="aicc_max_call_duration_side=BOT"/>
        <action application="hangup" data="NORMAL_CLEARING"/>
      </condition>
    </extension>

    <extension name="aicc_max_call_duration_agent">
      <condition field="destination_number" expression="^aicc_max_call_duration_agent$">
        <action application="set" data="aicc_hangup_cause=MAX_CALL_DURATION"/>
        <action application="set" data="aicc_max_call_duration_side=AGENT"/>
        <action application="hangup" data="NORMAL_CLEARING"/>
      </condition>
    </extension>

    <!--
      Queue extensions. The bot transfers a caller here when it calls
      transfer_to_agent, and aicc_inbound falls back here when the bot cannot
      take a call at all. Which queue serves which extension is a database
      question, so the script answers it.
    -->
    <extension name="aicc_queue">
      <condition field="destination_number" expression="^(7\d{3})$">
        <action application="lua" data="aicc_queue.lua $1"/>
      </condition>
    </extension>

    <!--
      One agent phone calling another.

      The two exports are what the application needs and what the stock rule
      never gave it.

      aicc_parent_channel names the channel this bridge was raised for, so the
      second leg can be recognised as the other half of the first one's call.
      It cannot be a call id: the caller's CHANNEL_CREATE reaches the
      application before this dialplan runs, so the caller can never carry its
      own identity in time — whichever leg the identity is minted on, the other
      one has already been adopted into a call of its own. A pointer back to
      the first leg has no such race, and it is the mechanism mod_callcenter
      already supplies for queue deliveries (cc_member_session_uuid).
      export puts the value on both legs; the leg that names itself is the
      caller, which is how they are told apart.

      Without it the second leg is adopted into a provisional call of its own,
      becomes that call's first party, and is therefore rendered ORIGINATOR and
      DIALING — the agent being rung is shown dialling the person calling them.

      aicc_call_type says INTERNAL where the switch would only see an outbound
      leg and guess OUTBOUND — the type is decided here, where the destination
      is still known to be an extension.

      continue_on_fail is false on purpose. An internal call nobody answers is
      an internal call nobody answered; there is no voicemail to fall into and
      no successor leg to write a second record for.

      aicc_track.lua names the agent being called, so mod_callcenter stops
      offering them queue calls while their phone rings. It has to be a script
      because only the database knows which agent is at an extension, and it
      has to be here because the leg this decorates is raised *towards* that
      agent: a directory entry's variables reach the channels a user raises,
      never the ones raised at them (measured). The caller's own leg is covered
      by their directory entry for the same reason, from the other side.

      aicc_bleg_vars is the {…} prefix it builds, empty for an extension no
      agent works at — which leaves the bridge string exactly as it was.
    -->
    <extension name="aicc_internal">
      <condition field="destination_number" expression="^(10[0-9][0-9])$">
        <action application="export" data="aicc_parent_channel=${uuid}"/>
        <action application="export" data="aicc_call_type=INTERNAL"/>
        <action application="set" data="hangup_after_bridge=true"/>
        <action application="set" data="continue_on_fail=false"/>
        <action application="set" data="call_timeout=30"/>
        <action application="set" data="ringback=${us-ring}"/>
        <action application="lua" data="aicc_track.lua $1"/>
        <action application="bridge" data="${aicc_bleg_vars}user/$1@${domain_name}"/>
      </condition>
    </extension>

    <!--
      The platform's own numbers, dialled from an agent's phone.

      Numbers in luacc.dids are routed in the public context, where a carrier
      delivers them. An agent dialling one (click-to-dial, or the keypad) is in
      this context, so without this rule the call finds no route or, with a
      trunk rule below, goes out to a carrier that does not own the number.

      aicc_did_route.lua asks the database, while the dialplan hunts, whether
      the number is an enabled DID; if so the leg is typed (OUTBOUND, unless
      whoever placed it already said) and transferred to it in public:
      the same doorway, the same aicc_inbound.lua, the same flow as a caller
      from outside. Any other number fails the second condition and the hunt
      goes on to the rules below, untouched.

      It sits before the include on purpose: a deployment's trunk rules in
      aicc/*.xml come after this one and so never see a platform number.
      Queue and agent extensions are matched above and never reach it.
    -->
    <extension name="aicc_platform_number">
      <condition field="destination_number" expression="^\d{3,20}$" break="on-false"/>
      <condition field="${lua(aicc_did_route.lua ${destination_number})}" expression="^true$" break="on-false"/>
      <!--
        A call is INTERNAL only when it rings a registered extension; a
        platform number is not one, so this call is OUTBOUND. A phone's own
        INVITE carries no aicc_call_type, and after the transfer the channel
        is in public, where an untyped call would be taken for INBOUND, so it
        is named here. A type stamped at creation (click-to-dial) is kept:
        break="never" lets an already typed call skip this.
      -->
      <condition field="${aicc_call_type}" expression="^$" break="never">
        <action application="set" data="aicc_call_type=OUTBOUND"/>
      </condition>
      <condition field="destination_number" expression="^(\d{3,20})$">
        <action application="transfer" data="$1 XML public"/>
      </condition>
    </extension>

    <!--
      Where a deployment adds what only it has: this laptop's simulated PSTN
      trunk, somebody else's carrier, a feature code a particular floor needs.

      It has to be an include rather than a second file declaring this context
      again. Two files that both open <context name="aicc"> do not merge — the
      first is used and the second is silently ignored, so its rules are
      visible to xml_locate and unreachable to a call. That cost an afternoon:
      a trunk rule sat in a second file, every outbound click-to-dial reached
      the dialplan and found nothing, and the leg hung up NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION
      while the rule appeared to be right there.
    -->
    <X-PRE-PROCESS cmd="include" data="aicc/*.xml"/>

  </context>
</include>
